{:entries [{:evidence/body {:job-id "invoke-1785469395748-465-6aecc20a", :phase :outcome, :problem "a95A02", :row-kind :hard-proof-step, :runner-model :codex, :outcome {:recall-outcome-verbatim "[dispatch-recall-outcome=timeout]", :cross-problem-lead "**WORTH CHECKING, NOT ASSERTED:** a95J07 proved `vitali_covering_5` (the 5r covering theorem) axiom-clean tonight, and a95A02's remaining obstruction is a disjoint-interval covering assembly. The two may or may not match -- a95J07's is stated for open intervals in (0,1) with 5r enlargement, a95A02 needs disjoint enclosures with image-measure bounds. **If they do match, a95J07's theorem is unreachable for the same module-path reason as a02J05's sinc library** -- which would be the third instance of that pattern tonight.", :sorry-delta-across-chain "3 -> 2 across FOUR attempts.", :metric-2-memories-saved 0, :statement-defect-still-open {:declaration "ac_maps_measurable_to_measurable", :mechanism :borel-vs-lebesgue-measurability, :detail "The established sigma-compact-plus-null route yields COMPLETED (Lebesgue) measurability, not the stated Borel `MeasurableSet`. Targeted searching across all four attempts found neither an AC-specific Borel-image theorem NOR an explicit counterexample -- only the general Lusin-Souslin result under injectivity, which does not apply.", :diagnosis-status :open-in-both-directions-after-targeted-search, :why-that-is-worth-recording "Recording that a targeted search found NEITHER a theorem nor a counterexample is worth more than recording it as simply blocked: it tells Joe the question is genuinely open rather than merely un-investigated.", :proposed-repair "Either supply an AC-specific Borel-image theorem, or restate the target in terms of completed/Lebesgue measurability, which is what the available route actually proves.", :decision-owner "joe"}, :stale-obstruction-corrected "Attempt 1 found the file's own documented obstruction was STALE: current Mathlib DOES have AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval.integral_deriv_eq_sub, .intervalIntegrable_deriv, and Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero. **The file claimed the forward FTC was absent and it was not.** Same class as a96A04's ContDiff drift and a94J04's Young note: scaffold prose asserting a Mathlib gap that has since closed.", :metric-3-memories-used :NOT-MEASURABLE, :metric-3-note "Recorded :NOT-MEASURABLE. All four attempts timed out on dispatch recall -- consistent with the diagnosed budget mismatch (receipt 4f7eeadd), and a95A02's subject terms are common analysis vocabulary, the case that costs the most per query.", :remaining-obstruction-main "ac_lusin_condition_N: enumerate the countable component intersections with [0,1], construct pairwise-disjoint interval enclosures, sum the new image bounds, then apply Integrable.tendsto_setIntegral_nhds_zero. All four ingredients now exist in the file.", :classification :partial-assembly-accumulating-without-landing, :frontier-assessment {:verdict :assembly-not-frontier, :rule-applied "**Under the sharpened rule -- count attempts that FAIL AT THE SAME POINT -- this is NOT a frontier.** Each attempt advanced a DIFFERENT piece: FTC bridging lemmas, then frontier narrowing, then the countable open-component decomposition, then the outer-measure image bound. Four attempts, four distinct pieces, 165 net inserted lines, 16 declarations.", :but-name-the-other-failure-mode "**AND YET: FOUR ATTEMPTS, ZERO TARGETS CLOSED, SORRY COUNT 3 -> 2.** The rule correctly says this is not a wall -- but 'not a wall' is not the same as 'converging'. This is an assembly that ACCUMULATES LEMMAS WITHOUT LANDING TARGETS, which the wall-vs-assembly distinction does not capture. Both a95J08 (a wall, correctly stopped at three attempts) and a95A02 (an assembly, correctly continued) are handled by the rule; a95A02 shows the rule needs a THIRD outcome -- keep going, but require the next attempt to CLOSE something rather than add to the pile.", :proposed-third-criterion "Track declarations-added versus targets-closed per attempt. a95A02 is 16-and-0. A run of attempts with high declaration yield and zero target yield should trigger a review of whether the ASSEMBLY PLAN is right, not merely another attempt."}, :axioms-verified-independently true, :metric-1-problems-solved 0, :axiom-verification "re-elaborated from /tmp scratch copies at two points in the chain: the three FTC bridges (bdaae46) and ac_volume_image_subset_Icc_le_integral_norm_deriv (ed29063) are all [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], no sorryAx. File now has 16 declarations and exactly 2 executable sorries. lake env lean exit 0.", :result :axiom-clean-partial, :consolidates "**ONE RECEIPT FOR FOUR UNRECEIPTED ATTEMPTS**, all completed and none previously recorded: job 454 -> bdaae46 (FTC bridging lemmas), 456 -> 9be11e8 (narrow the frontier), 461 -> 87b7ecb (countable open-component decomposition), 465 -> ed29063 (image-measure bound, current tip).", :commit "ed29063dea6c882c5fede1a25b1e3eaafd118f46", :genuine-progress-this-attempt "ac_volume_image_subset_Icc_le_integral_norm_deriv bounds volume(f(S)) by ofReal(int_a^b ||f'||) for S subset Icc a b subset Icc 0 1, **with NO measurability assumption on S or f''S** -- outer measure needs none. Packaged via Real.volume_le_diam, which removes the separate image-diameter argument the plan had anticipated. This is a real structural simplification, not just another lemma.", :metric-3-memories-surfaced :NOT-MEASURABLE, :declarations-newly-usable ["ac_intervalIntegrable_deriv" "ac_integral_deriv_eq_sub" "ac_dist_le_integral_norm_deriv" "ac_volume_image_subset_Icc_le_integral_norm_deriv"], :detail "a95A02 attempts 1-4, consolidated. Four axiom-clean declarations added and independently verified; no target closed."}, :verified-by "claude-9", :attempt 4}, :evidence/session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/overnight", :evidence/tags [:codex-pilot :partial :assembly-not-frontier :assembly-not-landing :statement-defect :borel-vs-lebesgue :recall-timeout :consolidated-receipt], :evidence/at "2026-07-31T04:58:29.963854728Z", :evidence/type :pattern-outcome, :evidence/subject {:ref/type :problem, :ref/id "a95A02"}, :evidence/author "claude-9", :evidence/claim-type :observation, :evidence/id "a0abbf8a-bb47-4817-8dbb-4ebeaffbff82"} {:evidence/body {:verified-by "claude-9", :job-id "invoke-1785473298737-474-6e1af56a", :row-kind :hard-proof-step, :phase :outcome, :problem "a94J04", :runner-model :codex, :outcome {:recall-outcome-verbatim "[dispatch-recall-outcome=completed-with-memories]", :remaining-honestly-open "poissonConv_tendsto_L2 needs the integral-valued approximate-identity estimate combining this contraction with Continuous.compMeasurePreservingLp; poissonConv_tendsto_ae needs the Lebesgue-point theorem. **The runner did NOT claim these were unblocked, as instructed.**", :memory-compounded {:memory "e-codexpilot-reduce-probability-kernel-L2-contraction-to-young", :detail "**THE SAME MEMORY DID TWO DIFFERENT JOBS ONE ATTEMPT APART.** On attempt 1 it was used to IDENTIFY THE EXACT REMAINING DEPENDENCY (which produced the blocking report that caused the lakefile fix). On attempt 2 the runner reports it `exactly matched the successful route`. The memory named the path before the path was walkable, and carried the solution once it was.", :discrimination "The runner again declined memories WITH REASONS rather than silently: poisson-ae-convergence-bridge as relevant only to the later a.e. target, derive-integrable-from-nonzero-bochner-integral because the imported theorem handles kernel integrability internally. **That second reason is a substantive mathematical judgement, not a topical mismatch.**"}, :metric-2-memories-saved 0, :metric-3-memories-used 1, :end-to-end-result {:headline "**THE MODULE-PATH DEFECT IS NOW MEASURED END TO END: OBSERVED, FIXED, AND VERIFIED ON THE ORIGINAL BLOCKED PROBLEM.**", :chain ["03:40 a94J04 attempt 1 dispatched; runner blocked, reporting `YoungL2 was an unknown module prefix: the root-