ude that convergence in SlitPath"], :defect-proof-status :argued-not-machine-checked, :false-targets ["logIntegral_im_eq_argStar_diff" "logIntegral_im_bounds"], :verification "working tree clean and last commit predates the run, as claimed; independently checked that the oscillatory term's absolute integral diverges via u = 1/t^2 giving u^(-1/2)|cos u|", :classification :blocked-all-targets-false, :metric-1-problems-solved 24, :arxiv-arm-entered false, :counterexample "theta(t) = t*(2+sin(1/t^2)), gamma(t) = pi*exp(i*theta(t)), gamma(0)=pi", :repair-applied false, :sorry-delta-headline 0, :no-commit true, :arm-that-paid :components-diagnosis-only, :metric-3-exclude-from-benchmark true, :declarations-newly-usable 0, :why-decisive "every applicable Mathlib FTC declaration explicitly requires interval integrability or absolute continuity; the scaffold omits the hypothesis every route demands", :repair-deferred-to :joe, :detail "a02J07 BLOCKED: BOTH targets are FALSE because SlitPath does not require gamma'/gamma to be interval-integrable, nor gamma to be absolutely continuous. Sorries 2 -> 2, no files changed, no commit -- verified: working tree clean, last commit touching the file predates this run. The runner diagnosed without touching anything, which is the sixth time tonight a runner has declined to repair a statement it was asked to prove. **THE COUNTEREXAMPLE IS EXPLICIT AND I CHECKED THE DIVERGENCE MYSELF.** Take theta(t) = t*(2 + sin(1/t^2)) and gamma(t) = pi*exp(i*theta(t)) with gamma(0) = pi. This satisfies SlitPath: gamma(0) = pi; for 0 < t <= 1 we have 0 < theta(t) <= 3 < pi so gamma avoids the nonnegative real axis; gamma is continuous on [0,1] and differentiable for t > 0. Then gamma'/gamma = i*theta' with theta'(t) = 2 + sin(1/t^2) - (2/t^2)cos(1/t^2). Substituting u = 1/t^2 turns the absolute integral of the oscillatory term into the integral of u^(-1/2)|cos u|, which DIVERGES -- so gamma'/gamma is not Bochner-integrable on [0,1] and intervalIntegral.integral_undef gives logIntegral gamma = 0. But the one-sided argument limit is 0 while argStar(gamma 1) = theta(1) = 2 + sin 1 > 0, so logIntegral_im_eq_argStar_diff asserts 0 = 2 + sin 1. The same path refutes logIntegral_im_bounds, whose first conclusion becomes 0 < |0|. CLASSIFICATION: the integral_undef / junk-value class, per scribe 32's taxonomy -- now a01A10, a01J01, a02J01, a02J03, a02J07, plus the earlier a95A08. PROOF STATUS: ARGUED, not machine-checked; the counterexample is described and mathematically sound but not formalised. WHAT MAKES THIS ONE PARTICULARLY CLEAR: the runner checked EVERY applicable FTC declaration -- intervalIntegral.integral_eq_sub_of_hasDerivAt_of_tendsto, integral_deriv_eq_sub, AbsolutelyContinuousOnInterval.integral_deriv_eq_sub -- and each explicitly REQUIRES interval integrability or absolute continuity. So the missing hypothesis is not an oversight in one lemma; it is the hypothesis every Mathlib route demands and the scaffold omits. REPAIRS offered: add IntervalIntegrable (fun t => deriv gamma t / gamma t) volume 0 1; or require absolute continuity of logStar composed with gamma; or define logIntegral as a convergent improper integral and put that convergence in SlitPath. Three materially different choices -- Joe's call."}, :recall-system :v3-typed-recall-status, :memory-use {:memory-use/status :outcome-recorded, :memory-use/surfaced-ids [], :memory-use/domain :mathematics, :memory-use/used-ids [], :memory-use/decision-id "invoke-1785460226070-427-f3121940", :memory-use/measurable? false, :memory-use/recall-outcome :timeout, :memory-use/runner-reported-status-verbatim? true}, :event :memory-use, :runner "codex-6", :dispatch-mode :duree-overnight-parallel}, :evidence/session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/duree", :evidence/tags [:memory :memory-use :memory-outcome :codex-pilot :duree-run :statement-false :junk-value-family :overnight], :evidence/at "2026-07-31T01:13:53.583276Z", :evidence/type :pattern-outcome, :evidence/subject {:ref/