S, BY PROBING THE SEAM RATHER THAN THE ENDPOINT.** a93J04 is eLpNorm-to-essential-supremum terrain and the corpus HOLDS a near-exact memory for it - prove-eLpNorm-to-essential-supremum-by-superlevel-and-probability-monotonicity, promoted in pass 11. Recall surfaced 0. I first hypothesised case-sensitivity, since subjects_for lowercases Lean identifiers and the query carried 'elpnorm' rather than 'eLpNorm' - THAT WAS WRONG and I checked it: eLpNorm, elpnorm and ELPNORM all return the same 2 memories. The actual cause: my receipts-crowding filter read :evidence/type at the TOP LEVEL of each search row, but rows are {:score _ :entry {...}} with the document NESTED under :entry, so the filter read nil every time, matched nothing, and fell back to unfiltered results. **That made TWO fixes inert simultaneously** - the filter never filtered, and because its fallback left every ladder tier non-empty, the ladder never fell through to the shorter queries it exists to reach. I had reported both as working, on the strength of measuring the ENDPOINT rather than the seam. Fixed (futon3c commit follows) and verified by direct probe: the pair tier 'tendsto elpnorm' now returns e-codexpilot-prove-eLpNorm-to-essential-supremum as its FIRST result, where the whole ladder previously returned 20 unfiltered rows at every tier. SEARCH: arm (a) carried it; Loogle surfaced tendsto_rpow_div though the final proof used Filter.Tendsto.nnrpow; LeanSearch DID return relevant declarations this time (eLpNorm_exponent_top, eLpNormEssSup, essSup_le_of_ae_le), breaking a five-run streak of returning nothing - worth noting against the promoted search rule rather than quietly ignoring. Arms (b) and (c) not needed."}, :recall-system :v1-enriched-ladder-interleaved, :memory-use {:memory-use/status :outcome-recorded, :memory-use/surfaced-ids [], :memory-use/domain :mathematics, :memory-use/error-time {:invocations 4, :hits 0}, :memory-use/used-ids [], :memory-use/relevant-memory-existed "e-codexpilot-prov