0, :detail "a01A04, DUREE RUN 4: AXIOM-CLEAN PARTIAL, 2 of 3 sorries closed (ballSlice_integral_tendsto_zero, ball_volume_superexponential_decay), file 3 -> 1, corpus sorries 170 -> 168. Clean count unchanged at 65, correctly - one sorry remains so the problem is not closed. THIRD VALIDATION OF ONE OF MY REPAIRS, and the cleanest: a01A04 was repaired this morning by replacing Fin n -> R with EuclideanSpace R (Fin n), following the precedent a95A03 already documents. The runner proved two parts AGAINST that statement using EuclideanSpace.volume_ball, so the repair was not merely sound but supplied exactly the API the proof needed - and there was NO repeat of the ContDiff-class error I made on a01A03. Verified independently: no deletion line in either commit touches a statement; all three targets still read EuclideanSpace R (Fin n); build exit 0; axiom check re-elaborated from a /tmp scratch copy for both new declarations -> [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], no sorryAx; sorry count via count_sorries.sh not grep; one file, two commits, tree clean apart from the pre-existing .bak. ROUTES: part (b) by Lebesgue dominated convergence, with the neat detail that n=0 defeats the compact-support dominator because 0^0=1, handled by applying dominated convergence to n+1 and shifting back with tendsto_add_atTop_iff_nat; part (c) by the explicit Euclidean ball-volume formula plus Gamma recurrence, V(2k) = pi^k/k!, V(2k+1) <= 2 pi^k/k! via k! sqrt(pi)/2 <= Gamma(k+3/2), split by parity and closed with exponential-over-factorial decay. BEHAVIOURAL: arm (a) components carried both proofs; arm (b) Zulip opened Iterated.20integrals.json, which records the historical ABSENCE of the measure-preserving Fin (n+1) product decomposition and recommends the Gaussian-volume method - a genuine negative-information hit, the archive telling the runner what is NOT available; arm (c) arXiv NOT ATTEMPTED and correctly so, third consecutive run where the cond did not fall through because the routes were already known. RECALL: 0 surfaced, and the cause is known and mine - the query was subjects_for's first three terms in statement order, 'ball volume recursion', where 'recursion' is a rare term that floors a conjunctive search. My earlier 0-to-8-hits measurement used a hand-picked term set and was therefore a hypothetical, not a measurement of the deployed fix. The ladder (triple -> pairs -> singles) was committed AFTER this dispatch, so run 5 is the first genuine compounding test. Notably the runner did NOT independently find the two relevant radial-measure memories either, so nothing compensated for the recall miss. REMAINING OBSTRUCTION is precisely named and has been carried forward as this row's :suggested-route, the first time that field has been populated from a partial rather than from a docstring: prove the integral of ballSlice^n over R equals the integral of sin(x)^(n+1) over [0,pi] via t = -cos x, including conversion of the whole-line indicator integral to [-1,1]; then reconcile the exact Wallis sine-power formulas with EuclideanSpace.volume_ball and Gamma recurrence to obtain the volume ratio. The runner states all required components exist and the remaining work is glue, with no axiom or proxy introduced."}, :recall-system :v1-enriched-capped, :memory-use {:memory-use/status :outcome-recorded, :memory-use/surfaced-ids [], :memory-use/query "ball volume recursion", :memory-use/domain :mathematics, :memory-use/error-time {:invocations 2, :hits 0, :note "both mandatory calls returned nothing"}, :memory-use/used-ids [], :memory-use/decision-id "invoke-1785415411984-319-e6e43466", :memory-use/unused-ids [], :memory-use/empty-reason :term-selection-by-statement-order-picked-a-rare-term}, :event :memory-use, :runner "codex-7", :dispatch-mode :duree}, :evidence/session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/duree", :evidence/tags [:memory :memory-use :memory-outcome :codex-pilot :duree-run :repair-validated], :evidence/at "2026-07-30T13:09:18.271824Z", :evidence/ty