OTH. In the current API the smoothness index is WithTop enat, where top = omega means REAL ANALYTIC, strictly above infinity; Mathlib's own ContDiff/Defs.lean states 'For n = omega, we require the function to be analytic'. Every compactly supported real-analytic function on R is identically zero by the identity theorem, so hidentity quantified over f = 0 ALONE and became vacuous, and g = 0 with h = 1 satisfies every hypothesis while refuting the conclusion. So a repair intended to remove vacuity INTRODUCED a different vacuity. Corrected to ContDiff R infinity (apm-lean 46cf809) and verified the repair is real by machine-checking that the smooth compactly supported test class is NON-VACUOUS - exhibited a ContDiffBump with f 0 = 1, exit 0. RUNNER BEHAVIOUR WAS EXEMPLARY and is the reason this was caught rather than worked around: it refused to weaken the target, preserved the statement and its honest sorry, and committed a MACHINE-CHECKED COUNTEREXAMPLE as two new declarations (analytic_compactSupport_eq_zero, convolution_identity_counterexample), both verified axiom-clean by ground control from a scratch copy - [propext, Classical.choice, Quot.sound], no sorryAx. It then named the exact one-token repair. Ground control verified statement integrity independently: the commit contains NO deletion lines touching the target, so my repaired statement was preserved and only new declarations were added. BEHAVIOURAL CHECKS, all pass, and one is the most interesting datum of the series: (B1) arXiv NOT RUN - and that is CORRECT, not a miss. The runner recorded that arm (c) did not fall through because the mathematical route was known and the statement was formally refuted during component assembly. That is precisely Joe's cond behaving as designed on its first live outing: the fall-through fires only when there is no route, and here there was one. (B2) Zulip PASS with a named thread path, 217875-Is-there-code-for-X/Calculus.20counterexamples.json, which corroborated the smooth-