nely valid, and the runner said so rather than blocking the whole file indiscriminately. WHY THIS MECHANISM IS THE MOST LIKELY TO BE SYSTEMIC: the other three witnessed defects are local slips -- a forgotten include, a missing ne-top, a pointwise-vs-a.e. confusion. This one is a TYPE-LEVEL modelling error. The statement is well-formed, compiles, reads correctly in English, and means something entirely different from what its author intended. Anywhere the corpus writes Fin n -> R and then uses Metric.ball, norm, dist, or Euclidean-flavoured geometry, the same defect is likely present -- so this is the highest-yield S9 grep of the four, and the one most likely to affect many rows at once rather than one. Secondary statement smell, not fatal: the exists f in part (a) is vestigial, since the displayed equality hardcodes ballSlice rather than using the bound f -- same shape as a00J06's vestigial rho. Verified: tree clean but for the pre-existing untracked .bak, HEAD unchanged at 8df8148, sorry count 3 -> 3, no commit -- as claimed. REPAIR IS JOE'S CALL: replace Fin n -> R with EuclideanSpace R (Fin n) consistently in (a) and (c)."}, :recall-system :v1-enriched, :memory-use {:memory-use/status :outcome-recorded, :memory-use/surfaced-ids [], :memory-use/domain :mathematics, :memory-use/used-ids [], :memory-use/decision-id "invoke-1785349810165-286-c097f49f", :memory-use/unused-ids [], :memory-use/error-time {:invocations 0, :hits 0, :note "no compile errors beyond the three expected sorry warnings"}}, :event :memory-use}, :evidence/session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/cron-lane", :evidence/tags [:memory :memory-use :memory-outcome :codex-pilot :cron-lane :faithfulness :hard-proof-step], :evidence/at "2026-07-29T18:34:01.753725Z", :evidence/type :pattern-outcome, :evidence/subject {:ref/type :apm-problem, :ref/id "a01A04"}, :evidence/author "claude-9", :evidence/claim-type :observation, :evidence/id "7739e43a-f9cf-4c15-950f-5c787f7ed8e4"} {:evidence/body {:recall-status :recall-e