0310-284-2e41ac48", :row-kind :hard-proof-step, :phase :outcome, :problem "a01A03-convolution-indicator", :runner-model :codex, :outcome {:verified-by "claude-9", :authorization :pending-joe, :runner-reason-incomplete true, :classification :blocked-statement-degenerate, :sorry-delta-real 0, :result :equivalence-to-unsatisfiable-hypothesis-machine-checked, :do-not-redispatch true, :faithfulness-ledger 5, :statement-change-required true, :vacuous-discharge-risk true, :divergence :degenerate-pointwise-conclusion-over-ae-constraints, :detail "a01A03 convolution_identity_implies_indicator: BLOCKED. Runner reached the correct DISPOSITION but on an incomplete reason, and review produced a sharper result. THE FINDING: g is a parameter and the conclusion is POINTWISE (forall x, g x = indicator x), while hidentity constrains g only through integrals, i.e. only almost everywhere. Ground control MACHINE-CHECKED (compiles, exit 0, /tmp/a01A03_probe.lean) that the theorem IMPLIES no integrable g satisfies hidentity: take any witness, modify it at 0, integrals are unchanged by a.e. congruence so the modified function is another witness, and its pointwise conclusion fails at 0 since 0 lies in Icc (-h) h whenever h > 0. The converse is immediate -- if nothing satisfies the hypotheses the statement is vacuously true -- so THE THEOREM IS EQUIVALENT TO 'hidentity IS UNSATISFIABLE'. It therefore says nothing whatsoever about g being an indicator, under any repair-free reading. WHERE THE RUNNER WAS INCOMPLETE: its two stated reasons are in tension and it did not reconcile them. Reason 1 (only a.e. equality is provable, so the statement is false) PRESUPPOSES a witness exists. Reason 2 (hidentity quantifies over every differentiable f without requiring an integrable derivative) is evidence that NO witness exists -- in which case the theorem is vacuously TRUE, not false, and 'blocked as false' would be the wrong verdict. Credit where due: the runner's a.e.-modification construction is the same one the probe formalises, and it refused to commit, which was right. WHY THIS ENTRY IS MORE DANGEROUS THAN LEDGER 3 AND 4: a FALSE statement is self-defending, since nobody can prove it. A DEGENERATE one is not. If this row is re-dispatched a competent runner may discharge it AXIOM-CLEAN by deriving False from hidentity, and it would enter the corpus as a solved row with a clean axiom witness -- the exact failure the announcement gate exists to prevent. :do-not-redispatch is load-bearing here in a way it is not for the two false rows. Verified: tree clean but for the pre-existing untracked .bak, HEAD unchanged at 8df8148, sorry count 1 -> 1, no commit -- all as claimed. REPAIR IS JOE'S CALL: make the conclusion an a.e. equality AND restrict the test-function class to one whose derivatives are integrable (e.g. smooth compactly supported); the runner's recommendation is right on both counts. RECALL: dispatch-time supplied no memories; offered half to be read against the post-fix series. Error recall correctly not triggered (no compile errors beyond the expected sorry warning)."}, :recall-system :v1-enriched, :memory-use {:memory-use/status :outcome-recorded, :memory-use/surfaced-ids [], :memory-use/domain :mathematics, :memory-use/used-ids [], :memory-use/decision-id "invoke-1785346210310-284-2e41ac48", :memory-use/unused-ids [], :memory-use/error-time {:invocations 0, :hits 0, :note "no compile errors beyond the expected sorry warning"}}, :event :memory-use}, :evidence/session-id "M-codex-sorry-loop/cron-lane", :evidence/tags [:memory :memory-use :memory-outcome :codex-pilot :cron-lane :faithfulness :hard-proof-step], :evidence/at "2026-07-29T17:35:11.841972Z", :evidence/type :pattern-outcome, :evidence/subject {:ref/type :apm-problem, :ref/id "a01A03"}, :evidence/author "claude-9", :evidence/claim-type :observation, :evidence/id "e8519ffd-7812-49f2-ae82-a015f50a9251"} {:evidence/body {:recall-status :recall-empty, :job-id "invoke-1785346210310-284-2e41ac48", :ph